INT. STRIKE ZONE LANES - LOCKER ROOM - NIGHT

Dented lockers. A bench scarred with initials. A single bulb swings over a pile of bowling bags. The sound of the league championship, the other team cheering, drifts through the wall like a taunt.

COACH WENDELL HUXLEY, 60s, in a polyester team jacket and bifocals, sits on an upturned bucket, clipboard on his knee. He does not look at it. DEXTER MALONE-ADEYEMI, 30s, a gangling anchor bowler, sits on the bench, hands hanging between his knees. ROMEO BALDERRAMA, 20s, a rookie, paces in front of the lockers, rolling a ball from palm to palm.

HUXLEY
Gentlemen.

ROMEO
Don't say it.

HUXLEY
I will say it.

DEXTER
He's going to say it.

HUXLEY
We lost.

ROMEO
(to himself)
We lost by six pins.

DEXTER
Seven.

HUXLEY
Seven. Thank you, Dexter.

ROMEO
It was the tenth frame.

DEXTER
It was always going to be the tenth frame.

HUXLEY
Nothing is always going to be the tenth frame.

ROMEO
Coach, I left the seven pin standing.

HUXLEY
I know.

ROMEO
I had it. I had the whole lane.

HUXLEY
You had a lane. You didn't have the whole lane. Nobody has the whole lane.

DEXTER
(to the room)
Does anyone have a beer?

HUXLEY
No.

DEXTER
Because I would like a beer.

HUXLEY
Dexter, you're thirty-four. Behave.

DEXTER
I'm behaving. I'm behaving like a man who just threw a four-ten split in front of his mother.

ROMEO
She was clapping.

DEXTER
She was clapping out of pity.

HUXLEY
She was clapping because you're her son.

DEXTER
Same thing.

A beat. Romeo laughs, short and ugly. Dexter starts too. Huxley fights it. Fails.

HUXLEY
All right. All right. Listen. Both of you. I have coached this team for twenty-two years.

ROMEO
Twenty-three.

HUXLEY
Twenty-three. And I have never once lost a championship by less than ten pins.

DEXTER
So this is progress.

HUXLEY
This is the closest we've ever been.

ROMEO
That's supposed to make me feel better?

HUXLEY
No. It's supposed to make you angry.

ROMEO
It does.

HUXLEY
Good. Hold that.

He stands. Hands each of them a towel from a pile. Dexter wipes his face. Romeo hangs his around his neck like a boxer.

HUXLEY (CONT'D)
(to the room, as to a crowd)
Next season, we come back. And we do not say sorry. We say hello.

DEXTER
Hello.

ROMEO
Hello.

HUXLEY
Louder.

DEXTER ^
HELLO!

ROMEO
HELLO!

Beyond the wall, the cheering dies. A janitor starts a floor-buffer. Huxley picks up his clipboard, crosses out something, and writes, in pencil: NEXT YEAR.
